            title: "Christ's courage in the Passion",
            summary:
                "Contemplate courage that feels sorrow, seeks companionship, prays honestly, refuses disordered force, and remains faithful in love.",
            opening:
                "What image of courage is corrected when Jesus openly says that his soul is sorrowful, asks friends to remain near, and prays that the cup might pass?",
            goal:
                "By the end, you should be able to read Gethsemane and Calvary without confusing holiness with emotional invulnerability, explain Christ's patience as loving freedom, and pray a feared duty within surrender to the Father.",
            terms: [
                "Passion — Christ's suffering and death freely accepted in obedient love for the salvation of the world.",
                "Agony — intense anguish or struggle; in Gethsemane it reveals real human suffering within faithful prayer.",
                "Obedience — free response to rightful truth and authority; Christ's obedience to the Father is loving communion, not servile coercion.",
                "Redemptive suffering — suffering united by grace to Christ's self-giving love; this never makes abuse, neglect, or preventable harm morally good.",
            ],
            chapters: [
                (
                    "Lecture I — Jesus does not perform invulnerability",
                    """
                    In Gethsemane Jesus names sorrow, falls in prayer, asks that the cup pass if possible, and asks chosen disciples to keep watch. Christian tradition does not treat these actions as failures of courage. They reveal the full humanity of the Son. Fear, anguish, bodily strain, desire for companionship, and petition can all exist within perfect fidelity. The opposite of courage is not visible emotion; it is abandonment of the good when one should remain free to choose it.

                    This scene protects believers from a counterfeit holiness that never admits need. Prayer does not require polished calm. A person can tell God exactly what is feared and desired. Asking for a burden to be removed is not rebellion. Seeking companionship is not a defect in faith. Jesus' friends fail to remain awake, yet his request itself dignifies human support. When support is absent or imperfect, the Father has not ceased to see, and the person may continue seeking appropriate help rather than turning isolation into a spiritual achievement.

                    “Not what I will, but what you will” is not passivity before every painful circumstance. Jesus knows the Father and the mission; he has taught, discerned, acted freely, and resisted earlier attempts to define his hour. A human being must not assume that another person's violence is God's will simply because it occurs. Discernment, moral truth, protection, and lawful help remain necessary. Surrender concerns fidelity to God within what cannot rightly be escaped, not submission to evil as though evil had divine authority.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture II — Patience born from greater love",
                    """
                    Pope Francis describes Jesus' patience during the Passion as the fruit of greater love rather than stoic resistance. Jesus does not answer insult by becoming an insulter or violence by delighting in violence. He refuses provocation, protects the truth of his mission, forgives, and entrusts himself to the Father. Patience here is morally active: evil does not get to reproduce itself inside his response.

                    Luke's account holds several relationships together. Jesus warns the mourners, promises paradise to the repentant criminal, prays forgiveness over his executioners, and finally entrusts his spirit to the Father. Mercy does not deny injustice; crucifixion remains evil. Christ's response reveals freedom that oppressors cannot control. For disciples, this does not mean every victim must speak forgiveness on demand or remain accessible to an offender. Forgiveness is grace, and safety, truth, justice, grief, and healing each have their own pace and responsibilities.

                    Christ's Passion also exposes the limit of toughness as an ideal. A hard person can endure pain by closing the heart. Jesus remains open—to the Father, the criminal, his mother and disciple in John's account, and the people he saves. Fortitude protects charity under assault. The question for the Christian is not merely, “Can I withstand this?” but “Can I remain truthful and loving without cooperating with evil?” Sometimes that will mean patient bearing; sometimes a firm no, escape, report, or defense serves the same love.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture III — The Cross, grace, and human suffering",
                    """
                    Christ's suffering is uniquely redemptive; no ordinary person repeats or completes his saving sacrifice by being harmed. Believers can unite unavoidable suffering with his offering, allowing pain to become a place of prayer, solidarity, repentance, and love. This union changes how suffering is borne without changing evil into good. Medical relief, protection, lament, legal action, and human support do not compete with the Cross when they serve dignity and truth.

                    Pray a feared duty using Gethsemane's pattern. First name sorrow and the desired relief. Second ask trustworthy companions to remain near. Third distinguish what can rightly be changed from what fidelity now requires. Fourth consent to the next act before the Father. Fifth receive bodily needs—sleep, food, treatment, breathing, movement—not as embarrassments but as part of creaturely obedience. This pattern is not a formula that guarantees calm. It keeps fear, desire, body, community, and mission in truthful relationship.

                    The Passion must be read through the Resurrection. Christian fortitude does not worship defeat. Jesus' self-giving passes through death toward the Father's victory, and hope insists that evil does not possess the final word. Many courageous acts remain visibly unfinished in this life. The cross teaches costly fidelity; the Resurrection prevents cost from becoming the ultimate meaning. The disciple can resist evil, accept unavoidable suffering, seek help, and hope beyond every outcome that remains outside human control.
                    """
                ),
            ],
            misconception:
