        lecture(
            id: "fortitude-justice",
            title: "Courage against evil and injustice",
            summary:
                "Give justice the strength to protect, report, resist, and witness without hatred, recklessness, or contempt.",
            opening:
                "Where do you already know what justice requires but hesitate because speaking or acting may cost approval, comfort, opportunity, or belonging?",
            goal:
                "By the end, you should be able to connect fortitude with justice, distinguish principled resistance from aggression, use authority and reporting channels proportionately, and understand daily witness within the horizon of martyrdom.",
            terms: [
                "Resistance — refusal to cooperate with evil and action to limit or correct it through morally fitting means.",
                "Solidarity — firm commitment to another's good that is willing to share cost rather than offer sympathy alone.",
                "Public witness — truthful action visible to a community when the good requires communal knowledge, accountability, or encouragement.",
                "Martyr — a witness who freely accepts death rather than deny Christ or choose grave moral evil; martyrdom is not suicide or sought danger.",
            ],
            chapters: [
                (
                    "Lecture I — Justice often needs borrowed strength",
                    """
                    Justice identifies what is due, yet fear can keep the will from giving it. A manager may know an employee must be protected, a citizen that a lie should be corrected, a friend that reputation is being harmed, or a professional that a report is required. Fortitude lends justice firmness when obedience brings cost. Without justice, courage can serve domination; without fortitude, justice can remain a conclusion never embodied.

                    Pope Francis describes fortitude as taking evil seriously and saying no to violence, slavery, oppression, wounds, and indifference. This no must be morally formed. Not every conflict is persecution, and not every preferred policy is the only just means. Gather facts, identify the person or common good threatened, understand your role, seek those most affected, and choose the fitting authority or action. Courage becomes solidarity when the person with greater safety or power shares cost rather than expecting the vulnerable person to stand alone.

                    Protection may include intervention, documentation, reporting, testimony, advocacy, refusal, lawful protest, financial choice, voting, professional discipline, or direct defense. The method depends upon competence and urgency. A bystander may safely interrupt ordinary cruelty but should call trained help during dangerous violence. A whistleblower may need legal guidance and evidence protection. An institutional leader has duties beyond private sympathy. Courage is not one style; it is firmness in the just good through the action actually entrusted.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture II — Refuse both cooperation and hatred",
                    """
                    Daniel 3 presents young men who refuse idolatrous command even under threat of death. Their answer combines trust in God's power with refusal to manipulate God: he can save, but even if the visible rescue does not come, they will not worship the idol. Their courage is clear about the act they cannot choose. They do not make their fidelity depend upon guaranteed survival.

                    Acts 5 shows apostles ordered to stop teaching, questioned, threatened, beaten, and released. They obey God rather than men while continuing their witness. Legitimate authority remains important, but no authority can make evil good. Resistance should normally use lawful and peaceful means, yet a command contrary to moral truth cannot bind conscience simply because disobedience has consequences. Formed conscience, accurate facts, pastoral and professional counsel, and willingness to accept responsibility protect refusal from becoming self-will.

                    Ephesians' language of armor locates struggle against spiritual evil and equips the disciple with truth, righteousness, readiness for peace, faith, salvation, the word of God, and prayer. The weapons are not hatred, slander, or dehumanization. Even force used in legitimate defense is governed by intention, necessity, and moderation. A courageous Christian can name an aggressor's act, protect victims, and support consequences while refusing revenge and lies. Evil is not overcome by installing its logic in the resistor.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture III — Witness from ordinary cost to martyrdom",
                    """
                    Veritatis Splendor presents martyrdom as the highest witness that moral truth cannot be betrayed even for survival. The martyrs do not treat death as a good to seek. They value life deeply and refuse a chosen evil when threatened with its loss. Their witness protects personal dignity because no circumstance authorizes using the human person or moral law as material for an outcome. Martyrdom is grace and vocation, never a reason to provoke danger or deny lawful escape.

                    Saint John Paul II immediately extends the lesson to ordinary Christian witness. Most believers will not face martyrdom, but everyone meets situations where fidelity has cost: lost approval, an opportunity refused, an apology made, a dishonest advantage surrendered, a victim supported, or a lonely truth maintained. These acts participate analogically in witness when they are rooted in Christ rather than self-display.

                    Public action should be chosen by the good, not the fantasy of persecution. Ask whether the facts are verified, private remedy has been attempted where safe and fitting, public knowledge is necessary, vulnerable persons consent where their story is involved, and the action can protect rather than expose them. Preserve evidence and use accountable allies. If the concern involves abuse, crime, discrimination, employment, or legal rights, qualified channels matter. Fortitude accepts real cost; it does not impose avoidable cost on other people in order to dramatize witness.
                    """
