            misconception:
                "Seeking help is not automatically courageous if the helper is unsafe, unqualified, or controlling, and receiving advice does not outsource conscience. Yet isolation is not spiritual strength. Prayer and sacraments can accompany medical, psychological, legal, recovery, emergency, and safeguarding care rather than replace them. In immediate danger, concrete protection takes priority over completing a reflection exercise. A trustworthy helper should enlarge truth and responsible freedom, not demand dependence, secrecy, money, affection, or obedience beyond the helper's real role and competence. Healthy support also welcomes appropriate questions, shared responsibility, second opinions, and review when the person's needs or circumstances change.",
