                    Spiritual supports form courage when used according to their nature. Scripture gives truth and memory; prayer opens desire and fear to God; the Eucharist unites the believer to Christ and his body; Reconciliation restores communion after sin; anointing strengthens the seriously ill; spiritual direction assists discernment; community carries burdens. These gifts can accompany therapy, medicine, legal aid, recovery programs, sleep, nutrition, and practical planning. Invoking grace against ordinary means can become presumption.

                    Ephesians' armor is received: truth, righteousness, readiness for peace, faith, salvation, God's word, and prayer. The believer stands within gifts, not self-manufactured armor. Build a rule that includes both action and reception. Name the skill you will practice, the sacramental or prayer support you will receive, the human helper you will contact, and the result you will entrust. Weakness no longer needs to be hidden to protect an image. It can become the truthful place where communion and faithful action begin.
