                    Begin with one situation, not a verdict on your personality. State the worthy good in concrete terms: a person protected, truth told, promise sustained, treatment entered, evil refused, or vocation served. Then name the feared losses without embarrassment—safety, health, income, belonging, reputation, comfort, control, or life. Fear often attaches to real goods, which is why courageous choice can be genuinely painful. The aim is not to shame fear but to prevent it from silently deciding.

                    Gather facts and distinguish present danger, probable consequence, imagined catastrophe, and remembered injury. Name your role and limits. What is actually yours to do? Who else holds authority or expertise? Which people depend upon your action, and which could be endangered by impulsiveness? Test motive: love, duty, anger, recognition, revenge, guilt, and thrill may coexist. Remove any means that depends upon deception, humiliation, needless risk, self-harm, or another moral wrong.

                    Decide whether the difficult good requires advance, endurance, retreat, or help. These are not ranks of courage. Advance begins action; endurance remains through unavoidable cost; retreat protects the good from needless danger; help joins your freedom to community and competence. The same situation may require all four in sequence. A person leaves danger, receives support, reports through a safe channel, and then endures a long process. Fortitude keeps each movement ordered toward the good.
                    """
