                (
                    "Lecture II — Build a graduated plan",
                    """
                    A small step should be safe enough to take now and meaningful enough to serve the good: document facts, schedule care, rehearse a sentence, ask an adviser, or establish a boundary. A medium step follows if the concern continues or capacity grows: formal reporting, supported conversation, structured treatment, public participation, or increased responsibility. A larger step belongs only when a named threshold is crossed and should include the authority and safeguards required.

                    Define stopping and emergency conditions. New threats, medical symptoms, retaliation, evidence of crime, danger to a child, or severe mental-health warning signs may require immediate professional action rather than the next planned step. On the other side, new evidence may show an accusation was mistaken or a method ineffective. Courage includes correction. A review date prevents anxiety from reopening every decision hourly while preventing stubbornness from carrying an obsolete plan indefinitely.

                    Prepare the body and environment. Sleep, food, movement, medication as prescribed, transport, documentation, finances, privacy, and a companion can change whether an action is possible. Pray, receive the sacraments where fitting, and ask others to intercede. These are not techniques for controlling God. They place the whole creature and community at the service of the good. State what result remains outside your power so faithful action is not confused with guaranteed success.
