                    First Corinthians closes with watchfulness, firmness in faith, courage, strength, and the command that everything be done in love. Second Timothy presents a life poured out, a race finished, faith kept, and hope fixed on the righteous Lord. Psalm 31 remembers distress, refuge, truth, mercy, and the call for all who hope in the Lord to take courage. These texts integrate action and reception, strength and love, present fidelity and final hope.

                    After an action, review process and effects. Was the good sound? Were facts sufficient? Did the means respect moral limits? Was help used? What cost occurred, and who bore it? Did the action protect or expose? What new fact requires repair or revision? A good act can meet a painful result; a reckless act can receive lucky success. Outcome matters, but it cannot alone judge courage. End the review with one lesson, one repair if needed, and the next faithful action.

                    Write a four-week rule: one truth to tell or boundary to practice, one duty to sustain, one support to receive, and one weekly prayer of hope. Keep the practices observable and modest. Record completion and reflection without calculating a courage score. Feeling more fear one week does not erase fidelity; feeling bold does not prove growth. Progress appears as greater freedom to serve the good truthfully, proportionately, lovingly, and with help. The course returns you to daily life where courage remains a gift to practice and receive.
